class.description:
[method NetwConnector.probe] answers one, and [NetwServerBrowser] caches the latest one per target so a browser row can render before anyone commits to joining. A probe never throws and never half-answers: every route out of it, including a null target and a transport that cannot probe at all, ends at one [constant STATUS_OK] .. [constant STATUS_INCOMPATIBLE] value, so a caller branches on [member status] rather than on whether it got an answer.
[member status] decides which of the other members carries anything. Only [constant STATUS_OK] and [constant STATUS_INCOMPATIBLE] carry an [member info], only [constant STATUS_OK] carries a meaningful [member latency_ms], and [member message] is diagnostic detail rather than text to show a player.
[codeblock]
var result := await NetwConnector.of(api).probe(target)
if result.is_ok():
    print("%d/%d players, %d ms" % [
        result.info.players, result.info.max_players, result.latency_ms,
    ])
elif result.status == NetwProbeResult.STATUS_INCOMPATIBLE:
    # info survives here, so the row can still show who is on the server.
    show_banner("That server runs a different build.")
[/codeblock]
The seven static factories are the only way to get a result whose status and payload agree, and a [NetwTransport] that answers a probe at all answers with one of them.
